Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time, the disciples came to Jesus and asked, "Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child and brought him to himself. He let the child stand among them.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't do this, you will never enter the Kingdom of Heaven."
Whosoever becomes free from pride like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Anyone who kisses a little child like this in my name is kissing me.
But what if one of these little ones who believe in me sins against them? If he does, it would be better for him to have a large millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.
How frightening it will be for the world because of the things that cause people to err! These things must come. But how frightening it will be for those who cause it!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or one foot than to enter the eternal fire with both hands and feet. For in hell, the fire burns forever.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to enter with two eyes and be thrown into the fire of hell. The Parable of the Lost Sheep
Look, don't underestimate anyone, these young ones. This is what I tell you. Their angels are in the sky, they can go at any time to visit my father who is in the sky.
<.>
What do you think? Suppose a man has 100 sheep and one of them goes astray. Isn't he going to leave the 99 sheep on the mountain? Isn't he going to go and look for the one that went astray?
What I am going to tell you is true. If that sheep is found, he will be happier with it than with the 99 who were not lost.
The same with your father in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to be lost. When someone sins like you did
Your brother wronged you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong to you. Keep it between yourselves. If he listens to you, you have reconciled with him.
But what if he doesn't want to listen to you? Then take along one or two others. The Scriptures say, "Every matter must be established by the testimony of two or three witnesses." (Deuteronomy 19:15)
But what if he also refuses to hear the witnesses? Then tell her to report it to the Church. And what if he also refuses to even hear the Church? Then treat him as you would treat a brother. Deal with him as you would deal with an unbeliever or with a tax collector.
What I am going to tell you is true. What you close on the ground will be closed on you in the sky. What you open on the ground will be opened on you in the sky.
Again, this is my advice to you. Suppose that two of you on Earth agree to anything that is asked of you. My Father in Heaven will do it for you.
Wherever two or three people are gathered in my name, I am still there with them." The servant who did not have compassion
Peter came to Jesus. He asked him, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who wanted to settle all accounts with his servants.
When the King started doing it, they brought him a man who owed him millions of dollars.
The man could not pay. Therefore, his master gave an order. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he had must be sold to repay his debt.
The server fell on his head in front of him. "Give me time," he asked. "I will pay back everything."
Sido felt sorry for him. He forgave him for what he had been and let him go.
But this slave went out and found one of the other slaves who owed him a few dollars. He grabbed him and started strangling him. He said to him, "Give me back what you owed me!"
The other servant fell on his head. "Give me a little time," he asked him. "I will pay you back."
But the first slave refused. Instead, he walked and fell down into the prison. He would stay there until he could pay back what he owed.
The other servants saw what happened. It made them very angry. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
Later, the master called the first slave. He said to him: "O evil slave, I forgive you for everything that you owed him, for the one you asked me about."
Didn't you have to show mercy to the other servant just as you showed mercy to me?
In his anger, Monsieur Diallo paid the guards to punish him. He will be punished until he pays back everything he owes.
This is how my father will treat each of you in heaven, unless you truly forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus spoke these words, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He treated them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any and every reason?"
Jesus said, "Have you not read that from the beginning the Creator 'made them male and female'? (Gen 1:27)"
He said, 'For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and be united to his wife, and they will become one flesh.' (Genesis 2:24)
What God has joined together, let man not separate.
"Solomon said, 'Why did Moses command that a man can give his wife a certificate of divorce and send her away?'"
Jesus said, "Moses allowed you to send away your wives because you were being stubborn. But it was not like that from the beginning."
Here is what I tell you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man can only divorce his wife if she was not faithful to him.
The students said to him, "If this is the way between a man and a woman, it is better not to marry."
Jesus replied, "Not everyone can accept the idea of staying single. Only those who are helped to live without marrying can accept it."
Some men are unable to have children because they were born that way. Some of them were made that way by other people. Others made themselves that way in order to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever is able to accept living that way should do it." The little children were brought to Jesus.
Some people brought little children to Jesus. They wanted him to reach out, lay his hands on the children, and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he left from there. Jesus and the rich young man.
A man came to Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to gain eternal life?"
Why are you asking me what is good? Isa answered. There is only one who is good. If you want to enter the kingdom, obey the commandments.
"Which of them?" the man asked. Jesus said, "'Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony.
Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) and "love your neighbor as yourself." " (Leviticus 19:18)
I have kept all these commandments, said the young man. What else do I need to do?
Jesus answered and said, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell what you have and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young master heard this, he went away sad. It was too precious.
Then Jesus said to His disciples, "What I tell you is true. It is hard for rich people to enter the Celestial Kingdom."
Again I tell you, it is difficult for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven. For even a camel will struggle to pass through the eye of a needle. But I tell you, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich person to enter the kingdom of God.
When the seekers heard this, they were very astonished. They asked, "Then, who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "What is impossible for people is possible for God."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything to follow you! What will be our reward?"
"The words that I speak to you are spirit, and they are life." Jesus said to them, "Believe me that I am in the Father and the Father is in me; or else believe me for the sake of my works. Believe me that I am in the Father and the Father is in me; or else believe me for the sake of my works. Do not think that I will accuse you before the Father; the one who accuses you is Moses, in whom you have hope. If you believed Moses, you would believe me, for he wrote about me. Yet despite that, you do not believe me, which means that you do not believe at all that what I teach comes from God. For if you truly believed that I came from God, you would believe what I teach. For I do not speak on my own; rather, the Father who lives in me does his works. Believe me that I am in the Father and the Father is in me; or else believe me for the sake of my works. For the one who does not believe in me is not rejecting me only; he is rejecting the one who sent me. For I did not speak on my own; the Father who sent me is the one who told me what to say and what to speak. And I know that his commandment is eternal life; the words that I teach are what God wants me to teach. And the Father who sent me is the one who testifies about me. You have never heard his voice or seen what he looks like, so you do not believe. You do not believe because you are not my sheep; my sheep hear my voice and follow me. I give them eternal life, and they will never perish; no one will snatch them out of my hand. My Father, who gives them to me, is greater than I am; and so he secures them permanently in the salvation that comes through trusting in him. And this is the will of him who sent me, that I should lose nothing of all that he has given me, but that I should raise it up on the last day. For this is the will of my Father, that for their sakes I should lose nothing until the final day.
Everyone who has come out of their homes, or their families, or their fields because of me will receive one hundred times more. And they will gain eternal life.
But many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The Tale of the Workers in the Field
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who had land. He went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
He agreed to give them the wage for a day's work. Afterwards, he sent them to his vineyard.
At about nine o'clock he went out again. He saw others standing in the marketplace and doing nothing.
He said to them, "You too, go and work in my vineyard. I will give you what is right."
They left. "He went out again with his back and at three o'clock and did the same thing.
Around five o'clock, he went out and found others standing close together. He asked them, "Why do you stand here all day long and do nothing?"
Since no one appointed us, they answered them. He said to them, "You too, go and work in my vineyard."
In the evening, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the person in charge of the workers. He said to him, "Call the workers and pay them. Start with the ones whose work was the last, and then finish with the first."
The workers who were hired around noon arrived. Everyone received their usual day's wage.
Those who arrived first, expecting to receive more, were disappointed when they each received the pay for an ordinary day.
When they got it, they started complaining about the owner.
These people who were just recently hired worked for only one hour, they said. You gave them the same pay that you gave us, we did most of the work and were under the scorching sun all day long.
The owner answered one of them. He said to him: "Friend, I am being fair to you. You don't agree to work for the normal day's pay?"
Take your money and leave. I wanted to give the people I appointed last time the same salary I gave you.
Don't I have the right to dispose of my money as I wish? Do you feel cheated because you freely gave to others?
Those who will be last will be first, and those who will be first will be last. Jesus is re-teaching us about his death that is coming.
Jesus was going up to the Feast of Tabernacles. So on the way, He took along His twelve disciples and He stood them on the side, in order to speak with them.
"We are going up to Jerusalem," he said. "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the teachers of the law. They will condemn him to death."
From then on, they will hand him over to the non-Jewish people. The people will mock him and flog him with whips. They will nail him to a cross. On the third day, he will rise from the dead!" A mother makes a request of Jesus.
The mother of Zebedee's sons came to Jesus with her sons. Kneeling before him, she asked for a favor.
What do you want?" Jesus asked. "Promise me," she said, "that one of my sons will sit at your right in your kingdom. Promise me that the other will sit at your left."
You do not know what you are asking, Jesus said to them. "Can you drink the cup of suffering that I am going to drink?" "We can," they answered him.
Jesus said to them, "You will certainly drink from my cup. And yet it is not for me to decide who will sit at the right or left hand of mine. Those places belong to those for whom my Father has prepared them."
The other nine students heard about this. They were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus gathered them and said to them, "You know the rulers of the Gentiles. They lord it over their people. Their great ones exercise authority over them."
Don't be like that. On the contrary, anyone who wants to be important among you must be your slave.
And anyone who wants to be first must be your slave.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give His life as the price for the liberation of many people. Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and His disciples were going to rest. A large crowd followed them.
Two blind men were sitting by the roadside. They heard that Jesus was passing by. So they called out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
The crowd told them to stop. They told them to be quiet. But the two men shouted even more, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stood up and called out to them, "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked.
Sir, they answered, we want to be able to see.
Jesus perceived a great deal of anxiety in them. He touched their eyes. Immediately they were able to see. And they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
When they all drew near to the city of Jerusalem, they came to the village of Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two disciples, saying to them, "Go into the village ahead of you; there you will find a colt tied, which no one has ever ridden."
He told them, "Go to the village in front of you. When you arrive, you will find a donkey tied up, and its foal will be with it. Untie them and bring them to me."
Unless someone tells you something, tell them that the Lord needs them. The owner will send them immediately.
This thing came to pass that what the prophet spoke might be a reality. It says,
Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion! Shout, O daughter of Jerusalem! Behold, your king is coming to you; righteous and having salvation is he, humble and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey.
The disciples went and did what Jesus had told them to do.
They brought the donkey and its foal. They spread their garments on them. Then Jesus sat on the garments.
A large group of people laid their carpets in the street. Others cut branches from trees and spread them in the street.
Some people went ahead of him, and some followed. All were shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered the city of Jerusalem, the entire city was shaken. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus. He is the Prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple. He began to drive out all those who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of the moneychangers. And he even turned over the seats of those who were selling doves.
He said to them, "It is written, 'The Lord has said, "My house shall be called a house of prayer."' (Isaiah 56:7) But you are making it a den of robbers." (Jeremiah 7:11)
The blind and lame came to Jesus at the temple, and there he healed them.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful things he did. And they saw the children in the temple area shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" But when the chief priests and teachers of the law saw this, they began to be very angry.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you never read in the Scriptures: 'The very young women and the children praise You?'" (Psalm 8:2)
From that time on, Jesus withdrew from among the people and went out to the region of Judea, to the town of Bethany beyond the Jordan. He spent the night there. And behold, a fig tree was there, in full leaf. And He came, seeking fruit on it, and found none. And He said to it, "Never again shall a thing come from you." And immediately the fig tree withered away. And when the disciples saw it, they marveled, saying, "How did the fig tree wither so quickly?" And Jesus answered, "Assuredly I say to you, if you have faith and doubt not, you shall move mountains. And nothing will be impossible for you." But this kind does not go out except by prayer and fasting. And while they continued to ask Him, He stood and said to them, "Let Me ask you something. Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath, or not?" And they began to argue with Him. And He looked at them and said, "What kind of generation are you? What kind of example are you setting? For you are not setting a good example, and you are not teaching the truth. For you are like children who play games and pretend to be what they are not. For you are not truly righteous, and you are not truly devoted to God. For your righteousness is like a filthy rag, and your devotion to God is like a worn-out garment. For you are not truly seeking God, and you are not truly desiring to do His will. For you are like a person who stands at a distance from a great king, and who shouts out to him, saying, 'I am your friend, and I am your servant. And I will do whatever you ask me to do.' But the king knows that this person is not truly his friend, and he knows that this person is not truly his servant. For the king can see that this person is not truly desiring to do his will, and he can see that this person is not truly seeking to please him. For the king knows that a true friend and a true servant is someone who is not only willing to do what is asked of them, but who is also truly desiring to do the will of the one they serve, and who is tru