Early in the morning, Jesus was returning to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the road. He walked up to it, but found nothing on it but leaves. And then he said to it, "Henceforth shall you never bear fruit!" And immediately the fig tree withered.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed. "How did the fig tree wither so quickly?" they asked.
Jesus answered and said, "What I tell you is true. You must have faith and not doubt. And then you will be able to do what they did to the fig tree. And you will be able to say to this mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea.' And it will be done."
"If you believe, you will receive what you ask for when you pray." Jesus' authority is called into question.
Jesus entered the temple court. As he taught there, the chief priests and the scribes came to him. They asked, "By what authority are you doing these things?"
Jesus answered and said, "I will ask you one question. If you answer me, then I will tell you from where I got these things."
Where did John's baptism come from? Was it from heaven, or from men?" They met to discuss this. They said, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will ask us, 'Why then do you not believe him?'"
But what if we say, 'Men'? We are afraid of people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet.
Jesus answered them, "I tell you that you do not know." Jesus said to them, "Therefore I tell you, you will never see the Kingdom of God." The story of the two sons.
What do you think about this? A man has two sons. He goes to the first son and says to him, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
He's not going, the son replied. But then he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The boy answered, "Yes, sir." But he did not leave.
Which of the two sons did what his father wanted? The first, Jairus. Jesus said to them, "What I tell you is the truth. Tax collectors and prostitutes will enter the kingdom of God before you."
John came to show you the proper way to live. And you did not believe him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes did believe. And you saw this. But even then you did not repent of your sins and believe him. The parable of the tenants.
He heard another story. A man had some land where he planted vines. He put a fence around it. He dug a pit for pressing the grapes. And he built a tower on it for observation. He rented the vines to some farmers. And then he went on a trip.
Menny, near harvest time, went to his tenant farmers to rent. He told them to gather his share of the fruit.
But the Carraways tightened Abido's bonds. They struck one of them. They killed another one. They threw stones at the third one to kill him.
Later, the man sent other workers to the tenants. He sent more than the first time. The tenants were treated the same way.
Finally, he sold his son to them. He said, "They will respect my son."
But Karawy saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will take possession of all the owner's property someday. Come on, let's kill him. After that, everything will be ours."
They took him and expelled him from the vineyard. Afterwards, they killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard comes back, what will he do to these tenants?
He will burn these evil people, he answered. Then he will fill the vineyard for other tenants. They will give him his share of the harvest at harvest time.
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read what the Scriptures say, 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone. This is the Lord's doing. It is marvelous in our eyes'? (Psalm 118:22,23)
Here is what I tell you. The kingdom of God will be taken away from you. It will be given to people who will produce its fruit.
Anyone who falls on this stone will break into pieces. But the stone will crush anyone who falls on it.
The priests and the deacons heard the stories of Jesus. They knew that he was talking about them.
They wanted to find a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowd. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
Tale of the Wedding Feast
Jesus told them more stories. He said,
Here is how the kingdom of heaven is like. A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sold his servants to the people who call them for dinner. The servants told them to come. But they refused.
Later on, he sold some of his wives. He told them, "Tell the guests that I have prepared my dinner. I have killed my bulls and my finest cows. Everything is ready. Come to the wedding feast."
But people didn't pay attention. One went to his field. Another went to his work.
The rest abducted his slaves. They treated them badly and then killed them.
The king was very angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed these criminals and burned their city.
And afterward the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is ready. But those I invited were not worthy to come."
Go to the street corner. Call out for dinner to anyone you can find.
Therefore the servants went out into the streets, and they gathered together all the people whom they could find, both good and bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king went in to see the guests. He noticed a man who was not dressed in the wedding garment.
Guest, he asked, how did you get in here without a wedding dress? The man couldn't find anything to say.
And afterward, the king said to his servants, "Tie his hands and feet. Cast him out into the darkness. There, people will weep and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. And what is right, to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out. They planned to entangle Jesus in his words.
"Sir," they said, "we know that you are a good man. You teach the way of God in truth. You do not let others tell you what to do or say. You do not care how important they are."
Tell us, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus was aware of their evil schemes. He said to them, "You false pretenders! Why are you trying to seize me?"
Show me the money that people use to pay taxes. They brought him silver money.
Ask them, "Whose image does it bear? And what are its words?"
From Caesar, they got an answer. And then he told them, "Give to Caesar what belongs to Caesar, and give to God what belongs to God."
When they heard this, they were astonished and went along with it, going even further. Marriage, when the dead come back to life.
On the same day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They did not believe that people rise from the dead.
The teacher said, "This is what Moses told us. If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's name."
There were seven brothers among us. The first got married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife to his brother.
The same thing happened to the second, third, and even seventh brother.
In the end, the woman died.
Now that the dead have stopped, hers will be the wife of whom? All seven of theirs were married to her.
Jesus answered and said, "You are mistaken, not knowing the Scriptures nor the power of God."
The dead will not die anymore, they will not get married. And their parents will not give them in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What do you think about those who died, do they rise? Haven't you read what God said to you?
He said, 'I am the God of Abraham. I am the God of Isaac. And I am the God of Jacob.' (Exodus 3:6) He is not the God of the dead, but of the living.'
When the disciples heard this, they were astonished at what Jesus had taught them. The greatest commandment is
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees were unable to answer Jesus. Now the Pharisees have gathered.
One of them was an expert in the law. Now he tested Jesus with a question.
"Professor," he asked, "what is the most important thing about the law?"
Jesus said, "Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul. Love him with all your mind." (Deuteronomy 6:5)
This is the first and most important thing.
And the second is like it: 'You shall love your neighbor as yourself.' (Leviticus 19:18)
Everything written in the law and the prophets is based on these two commandments." Son of whom is Christ?
The Pharisees gathered together. Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he? They brought him, "The son of David."
He said to them, "Why then does David call him 'Lord'? For the Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said,
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I put your enemies under your feet." (Psalm 110:1)
If David called him 'Mister', how could he be David's son?
No one could answer him a single word. From that day on, no one ever dared to ask him a question again.
Jesus judges the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus talked with the crowds and with his disciples.
He said, "The law teachers and the Pharisees sit in the seat of Moses,"
You have to obey them. Do everything they tell you to do. But don't do what they do. They don't practice what they preach.
They grab heavy loads and place them on the shoulders of other people. But they themselves don't want to raise even a finger to move it.
Everything they do, they do so that others may see it. On their foreheads and arms they wear small cases containing texts from the Holy Book. They make the cases very wide. And they keep the tassels of their coats very long.
They love to sit in the place of honor at dinners. They also love to have the most important seats in temples.
They like to be greeted in the marketplace. They like it when people call them 'Lord'.
But you must not be called 'Rabbi'. You have only one master, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth 'father'. You have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You should not be called 'Teacher'. You have one Teacher, the Christ.
The most important person among you will be your slave.
He who lifts his head will be brought down, and he who is brought down will be lifted up.
How much will it be for you, you teachers of the law and you Pharisees! You impostors! You lock people out of the kingdom of heaven. You yourselves do not enter, nor will you let those enter who are trying.
<.>
What a torment this is for you, you teachers of the law and you Pharisees! You hypocrites! You cross sea and dry land to make a single convert, and then you make the convert twice as much a child of hell as you yourselves are.
What a strange thing you are, you blind guides! You say, 'If someone swears an oath by the temple, it means nothing. But if someone swears an oath by the gold of the temple, they must do what they promised.'
You are blind and stupid! What is more important? Is it the gold? Or is it the temple that sanctifies the gold?
You say, "Even so, 'if anyone swears by the altar, there is nothing to it; but if anyone swears by the gift that is on the altar, he is bound to perform the vow.' "
O ye blind! What is the most important? Is it the gift? Or is it the altar that sanctifies the gift?
For anyone who swears by the altar, is swearing by it and by everything on it.
And anyone who swears by the name of the temple swears by it and by the name of the one who dwells in it.
And anyone who swears by heaven is swearing by the throne of God and by the one who sits on it.
