The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified."
He's not here! He got up, just as he said he would! Come and see the place where he was.
Quickly! Tell His followers, "He has been raised from the dead. He will go ahead of you to Galilee. There you will see Him." Now I have told you.
The women went quickly from the tomb. They were afraid, but they were very happy. They ran to tell the disciples.
Suddenly, Jesus found them. He said to them, "Peace!" They came to Him, touched His feet, and worshipped Him.
And afterward, Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards reported to the chief priests.
From me, the women were on their way, and some of the guards went to the city. The high priests were informed of everything that happened.
The chief priests and the elders met together, and they devised a plan. And they gave a large sum of money to the soldiers, saying, "You are to guard this tomb diligently."
They told the soldiers, "We want you to say, 'His disciples came at night. They stole His body while we were sleeping.'"
If the governor hears this report, we will pay him. This will protect you from trouble.
The soldiers took the money and did what they were told to do. This story spread all among the Jews until this day. The last commands of Jesus to his disciples
After this, the eleven disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain to which Jesus had called them to go.
From the moment they saw it, they worshipped it. But some of them still have their doubts.
Afterward, Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
You must go and teach all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey everything I have ordered you. And be sure that I am always with you, until the end.
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
Here is the beginning of the joyful news about Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote: "I send my messenger before you, who will prepare your way." (Malachi 3:1)
A messenger is calling in the desert, "Prepare the way for the Lord; make straight paths for him." (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. About the people in the desert. And he preaches to the people to get baptized and turn a new page and get away from their sins. And then God will forgive them.
All the people from the countryside of Judea went out to him. All the people from Jerusalem were baptized by him. When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the river Jordan.
John wore a garment made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. And he ate locusts and wild honey.
Here is what John was calling out. "After me, there will come one who is mightier than I. It is not enough for me simply to fall down and untie his sandals."
I will baptize you with water. But he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Jesus was baptized and tempted.
At that time, Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. John baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus was baptized in the water. At that moment, he saw the sky being torn open. He saw the Holy Spirit descending upon him like a dove.
A roaring voice came from heaven. It said to him, "You are my son, whom I love. I am so very pleased with you."
Immediately, the Holy Spirit sent Jesus into the wilderness.
He was in the desert for 40 days. Satan tempted him. The wild beasts did not harm Jesus. The angels ministered to him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was put in prison, Jesus went into Galilee. He was preaching the good news of God.
The time has come, he said. The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe the good news!
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. He met Simon and his brother Andrew there. They were throwing a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Come, follow me, said Jesus. I will help you to fish for people.
Immediately they emptied their window and followed him.
After this, Jesus walked a little way forward. When he did this, he saw James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat preparing their nets.
Immediately he called them. Leaving their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men, they followed Jesus. Jesus Expels an Evil Spirit
Jesus and those with Him went to Capernaum. When the Sabbath came, He went to the synagogue. There He began to teach.
The people were astonished at his teaching. He taught them as one having authority, and not as the scribes.
Suddenly a man in their synagogue shouted. He was possessed by an evil spirit. He said,
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Did you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!
Silence! Jesus said firmly. Come out of him!
The evil spirit shook the man violently. Then it came out of him with a scream.
All the people were amazed. They asked each other, "What is this? A new teaching! And with authority! He even commands evil spirits, and they obey him."
The news about Jesus spread rapidly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those with him went out of the synagogue. Immediately they went with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother was lying in bed. She had a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He walked over to her. He took her hand and helped her up. The fever left her. Afterwards, she began to serve them.
In the evening, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all who were sick. They also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They had all kinds of diseases. And he cast out many demons. But he would not let the demons speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus is praying in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning, and still dark. Jesus got up and went out of the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found me, they called out, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus replied, "Let's go somewhere else. I want to go to the neighboring cities. I need to preach there as well. This is the reason I came."
He traveled throughout the region of Galilee. He was preaching in their synagogues. And casting out demons also. Jesus heals a man who had a skin disease.
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. On his knees, he begged Jesus. He said to him, "If you are willing, you can make me 'clean'."
Jesus was filled with profound concern. He reached out His hand and touched the man. He said, "I am willing to do it." Tell Him, "Be clean!"
Immediately, the illness left him. He recovered.
Jesus sent him away immediately. He gave him a strong warning.
"Don't tell this to anyone," he said. "Go and show yourself to the priest. Make the offerings that Moses commanded. It will be a testimony to the priest and to the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and started talking right away. He spread the news to everyone. That's why Jesus couldn't enter the city publicly. He stayed outside in deserted places. But people still kept coming to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who couldn't walk.
A few days later, Jesus returned to Capernaum again. The people heard that he had returned home.
Many people gathered until there was no more room. Even outside the door there was no more room. And Jesus taught them the word.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't get the paralyzed man close to Jesus because of the crowd. So they dug a hole through the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on his stretcher through the hole.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some law professors were sitting there. They were thinking,
Why is this man talking like this? He's saying something extremely evil! Only God can forgive such sins!
Immediately Jesus knew in his spirit what they were thinking in their hearts. He said to them, "Why do you think these things in your hearts?"
Is it easier for me to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? or to say, "Get up, take your mat and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. After this, Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
"I tell you," he said, "get up. Take your mat and go home."
The man stood up and took his mat. Then he went out into the street, and everyone was looking at him. All the people were amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Once again, Jesus went out beside the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd gathered around him. He began to preach to them.
As he walked, he saw Levi, the son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting at the tax collector's booth. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." Levi got up and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that followed Jesus.
Some Pharisee teachers of the Law who were from Frees were here; they saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors; and then they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard about this. He said to them, "Those who are in good health do not need a physician; but those who are sick do need one. I did not come to call the righteous to repentance, but I came to call sinners to repentance." They asked Jesus about fasting.
John's disciples and the Pharisees used to fast. Some people came to Jesus. They said to him, "John's disciples often fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees also fast. But your disciples never fast. Why is it that your disciples do not fast?"
Jesus answered and said, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? As long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast."
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. And on that day they will go away without eating.
People do not patch a piece of new cloth onto old clothing. If you do, the new piece will unravel from the old. This will make the tear even worse.
People don't pour new wine into old bottles. If you do, the wine will burst the bottles. And then, the wine and the bottles will both be ruined. No, everyone who pours new wine into bottles must also pour new wine into new bottles." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath
One day, Jesus was walking with his disciples through the grainfields. The disciples began to pluck some of the heads of grain.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, this man is doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath. Why then do your disciples do what is unlawful on the Sabbath?"
Answer, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
At that time Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it. David even gave some to his men.
And after that Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for humankind. Humankind was not made for the Sabbath."
Therefore, the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath.
Healing on Saturday.
Again, Jesus went into the synagogue. There was a man there who had a withered hand.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were closely following him. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath day.
Jesus spoke to the man with the withered and deformed hand. He said to him, "Stand in front of everyone."
And after that, Jesus asked them, "What do the scribes say that the Law requires us to do on the Sabbath? Is it to do good or evil? Is it to preserve life or to destroy it?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them, angry. He was very upset because their hearts were stubborn. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was like new.
Then the Pharisees went out and conspired with the Herodians against Jesus, hoping to kill him. The crowds followed Jesus.
Jesus went to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples. A large crowd from Galilee followed him.
People heard about everything Jesus was doing. Many of them came to him. They came from Judea, Jerusalem, and from as far as Tyre and Sidon. They came from the region east of the Jordan River. Large crowds gathered to hear Jesus and to be healed of their diseases and sicknesses.
Because of the crowd, Jesus told his disciples to find him a small boat. This would prevent people from crowding him.
Jesus healed many people. So those who were sick were pushing to touch him.
People who had evil spirits saw him, fell down before him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve disciples.
Jesus went up on a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed 12 of them and called them apostles. From then on, they would be with him. And he would send them out to teach people.
They had the authority to cast out demons.
Why did Jesus appoint the twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus named them Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
They were Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James the son of Alphaeus, and Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He is the one who will betray him to his enemies afterwards. Jesus and Belzebul
Jesus entered the house. Once again a large crowd gathered. Their number was so great that Jesus and His disciples were not even able to eat.
His family heard about this. They went to take responsibility for him. They said, "He is crazy."
Some of the teachers of the law were there, who had come down from Jerusalem. They said, "By the prince of demons he is driving out demons!"
Now Jesus summoned them and spoke to them in parables. He said to them: "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
A kingdom fighting against itself cannot stand.
If the family is scattered, it cannot endure.
